---
import BodyFatCalculator from '@/components/calculators/BodyFatCalculator';
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How accurate is the Navy method?',
    a: 'Validation studies put its error at roughly ±3–4 percentage points compared with DEXA, similar to consumer bioimpedance scales and better than most visual estimates. Its real strength is tracking change over time with the same tape.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I measure correctly?',
    a: 'Use a flexible tape, snug but not compressing the skin. Neck: just below the larynx. Waist: men at the navel, women at the narrowest point. Hips (women): the widest point of the buttocks. Measure relaxed, standing, after a normal exhale.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a healthy body-fat percentage?',
    a: 'For men, 10–20% is lean-to-fit and up to about 24% is average. For women, essential fat is higher, so 18–28% is lean-to-fit and up to about 31% is average. Very low levels are not healthier and can disrupt hormones.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I use this instead of BMI?',
    a: 'Use both. BMI is a screening number; body fat tells you what your weight is made of. If BMI says overweight but body fat is in the fitness range, trust the body fat.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout
  slug="body-fat"
  title="Body-Fat Calculator"
  description="Estimate your body-fat percentage with a tape measure using the U.S. Navy method. Get lean mass, fat mass and your category, then save it to unlock better calorie estimates."
  lede="Estimate your body-fat percentage with nothing but a tape measure, using the U.S. Navy circumference method. See your lean and fat mass and where you sit on the fitness scale."
  faq={faq}
>
  <BodyFatCalculator client:load slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How the Navy method works</h2>
  <p>
    Developed by the Naval Health Research Center in the 1980s, the method uses a logarithmic
    regression on neck, waist (and for women, hip) circumferences and height. It was validated
    against underwater weighing across a large, diverse sample. It is the same test used for
    military fitness standards precisely because it is fast, cheap and repeatable.
  </p>
  <h2>Why body fat matters more than weight</h2>
  <p>
    The scale cannot tell muscle from fat. When you lose weight without resistance training, up to a
    quarter of the loss can be lean tissue. Tracking body fat alongside weight tells you whether
    your deficit is doing its job. Combine this tool with the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/protein/">protein calculator</a> and read{' '}
    <a href="/guides/strength-training-while-dieting/">why lifting while dieting</a> matters.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
